@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Complex composite-structure view — a nested platform with 12+ parts across collaborating classifiers, multiple ports, and provided/required interfaces.
'   usecase:      Teach nested structured classifiers, collaboration use, and ports with provided/required interfaces.
'   complexity:   complex
'   domain:       Platform / Runtime
' ============================================================================
title Example Composite Structure - Complex Platform Internals

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' The platform is modelled as a top-level structured classifier
' "ApplicationPlatform" that itself contains a nested structured classifier
' "RequestPipeline".  Ports expose typed interfaces; some ports are provided
' (the platform offers them) and some are required (the platform needs them
' from the environment).  A COLLABORATION USE shows a reusable pattern
' ("Caching") bound into the structure.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rectangle "ApplicationPlatform" <<structuredClassifier>> as platform {

  ' ===== Boundary ports ================================================
  port "in http : IHttpRequest"   as pHttp
  port "out resp : IHttpResponse" as pResp
  port "req auth : IAuthProvider" as pAuth   ' required — external
  port "req cache : ICache"        as pCache  ' required — external
  port "req db : IRepository"      as pDb     ' required — external

  ' ===== Top-level parts ===============================================
  component "router : Router"          as router
  component "ctrl : Controller"        as ctrl
  component "repo : RepositoryProxy"   as repo
  component "sink : AuditSink"         as sink

  ' ----- Nested structured classifier as a part -----------------------
  rectangle "RequestPipeline" <<structuredClassifier>> as pipeline {

    port "in req : IRequest"    as pReqIn
    port "out out : IResponse"  as pReqOut

    component "val : Validator"      as val
    component "authz : Authorizer"   as authz
    component "exec : Executor"      as exec
    component "xform : Transformer"  as xform
    component "serial : Serializer"  as serial

    val   --> authz : check
    authz --> exec  : run
    exec  --> xform : adapt
    xform --> serial : encode

    ' Delegation for the nested classifier's ports.
    pReqIn  --> val
    serial  --> pReqOut
  }

  ' ===== Collaboration use: a reusable Caching pattern ================
  ' The (caching : Caching) syntax denotes a collaboration USE — a bound
  ' occurrence of a reusable collaboration.  It is a part typed by a
  ' collaboration rather than a plain class.
  component "caching : Caching" <<collaborationUse>> as caching

  ' ===== Wiring between top-level parts ================================
  router --> ctrl  : route
  ctrl   --> pipeline : handle
  ctrl   --> repo   : persist
  ctrl   --> sink   : log

  ' ===== Delegation from boundary ports to parts =======================
  pHttp   --> router
  router  --> pResp
  authz   --> pAuth    ' required interface satisfied externally
  repo    --> pDb      ' required interface satisfied externally
  caching --> pCache   ' collaboration use binds to required ICache
}

note right of platform
  <b>Nested classifier</b>
  RequestPipeline is itself a structured
  classifier nested inside the platform.
  Its internal parts are invisible at
  the platform level except through its
  own ports (pReqIn / pReqOut).
end note

note bottom of platform
  <b>Collaboration use</b>
  "caching : Caching" is a bound instance of
  a reusable collaboration — a named
  pattern of roles.  It is how a
  composite-structure diagram references
  a pattern instead of redrawing it.
  <b>Provided vs required ports</b>
  pHttp/pResp are PROVIDED (platform
  offers them). pAuth/pCache/pDb are
  REQUIRED (platform needs them from its
  environment) — drawn the same way but
  annotated; the distinction is in the
  interface direction, not the glyph.
end note

@enduml
